{"id":"07-mutex","prompt":"Explain what a mutex is.","expect":"plain","events":[{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"message_start","message":{"id":"msg_07"}}},{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_start","index":0,"content_block":{"type":"text","text":""}}},{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":0,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"A mutex is a lock that lets one thread at a time"}}},{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":0,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" into a piece of code, so two threads cannot"}}},{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":0,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" change the same data together.\n\nIn Python it"}}},{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":0,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" looks like this:\n\n```python\nimport"}}},{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":0,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" threading\n\nlock = threading.Lock()\ncount ="}}},{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":0,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" 0\n\ndef add():\n    global count\n    with lock:\n "}}},{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":0,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":"       count += 1\n```\n\nWithout the lock, two"}}},{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":0,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" threads could both read the old value of"}}},{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_delta","index":0,"delta":{"type":"text_delta","text":" `count` and one increment would be lost."}}},{"type":"stream_event","event":{"type":"content_block_stop","index":0}},{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_07","model":"claude-fixture","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A mutex is a lock that lets one thread at a time into a piece of code, so two threads cannot change the same data together.\n\nIn Python it looks like this:\n\n```python\nimport threading\n\nlock = threading.Lock()\ncount = 0\n\ndef add():\n    global count\n    with lock:\n        count += 1\n```\n\nWithout the lock, two threads could both read the old value of `count` and one increment would be lost."}]}}]}
